  "slug": "time-controlled-criticality-bc5c",
  "summary": "Six preprints extend classical tensor-network, symmetry, and Pauli-propagation methods to larger fermionic, chemical, thermal, and scar-state problems.",
  "synthesis": "The items share a focus on classical structure, such as symmetry, tensor-network form, and learned or propagated representations, to make many-body problems tractable at larger scale or lower quantum cost. NN-GPEPS combines neural networks with Gaussian tensor networks for strongly correlated fermions, reporting state-of-the-art Hubbard energies. Two papers load DMRG matrix product states onto hardware by exploiting spin symmetry: abelian block-sparse protocols, with Toffoli counts cut up to 135x, and non-Abelian SU(2) reduction via Wigner-Eckart and Clebsch-Gordan transforms. PP-VarQITE pairs Pauli propagation with variational imaginary time for shallow thermal-state circuits on up to 98 qubits. Further items cover machine-learned scaling of holographic circuits and unified preparation of scar states from Dicke states.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-26"
}
